```

This uses Python's built-in `sorted()` (Timsort under the hood — O(n log n)), which is the right choice for almost all real-world cases. If you wanted to see the mechanics of sorting instead of just calling a built-in, here's a manual bubble sort version too:

```python
def bubble_sort(numbers):
    """Sort a list in place using bubble sort (O(n^2)) — educational, not for large lists."""
    arr = numbers.copy()
    n = len(arr)
    for i in range(n):
        for j in range(0, n - i - 1):
            if arr[j] > arr[j + 1]:
                arr[j], arr[j + 1] = arr[j + 1], arr[j]
    return arr


if __name__ == "__main__":
    numbers = [64, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90, 5]
    print("Bubble sorted:", bubble_sort(numbers))
```

